+\subsection{A centered core-to-surface response: HE~0435-1223 closure screen}
+\label{sec:he0435-core-pull}
+We distinguish the ordinary gravity of a compact central body from an
+additional response of the surface to core coupling. A quadratic model
+makes the missing input explicit. For a surface mode $q$, complementary
+fields $y$, and a prescribed core load $f_c$, let
+\begin{equation}
+ E(q,y)=\tfrac12 kq^2+q b^{\dagger}y+
+             \tfrac12 y^{\dagger}Ky-f_c^{\dagger}y .
+ \label{eq:he0435-core-load}
+\end{equation}
+In the real static reduction with positive $K$, eliminating $y$ gives a
+stiffness $k-b^{\dagger}K^{-1}b$ and a force
+$-b^{\dagger}K^{-1}f_c$ at $q=0$. Knowing the stiffness does not fix the
+force. In particular, the presence of a black hole by itself specifies
+neither $f_c$ nor the resulting weak-field optical potentials.
+
+We use the converged foreground galaxy--group calculation described
+above with a $1.5$ Mpc half-box and a $\pm800$ kpc integration window.
+We add a centered, attractive optical response as a diagnostic only:
+\begin{equation}
+ \delta\widehat{\boldsymbol\alpha}(\boldsymbol b)
+ =\frac{4G M_{\rm eq}(<b)}{c^2 b^2}\,\boldsymbol b ,
+ \label{eq:he0435-core-lens}
+\end{equation}
+where $M_{\rm eq}$ is a projected lens-equivalent mass, not measured
+matter or a source derived from Eq.~\eqref{eq:he0435-core-load}.
+The ordinary foreground profiles and rear G1 model are unchanged.
+The new response deflects both the four quasar paths and the path
+to the apparent G1 centroid, so their separations on the G1 plane
+are recalculated before back-projecting to the quasar plane.
+A free common source is eliminated by subtracting the centroid of the
+four back-projections.
+
+With the correction off, the unweighted source-plane scatter is
+$898.86$ mas. Minimizing that same scatter over one nonnegative
+amplitude, separately for three profiles, gives:
+\begin{center}
+\begin{tabular}{lrr}
+\hline
+Trial projected mass law & $M_{\rm eq}(<6.39\,{\rm kpc})$ &
+  Source scatter \\
+ & ($10^{11}M_\odot$) & (mas) \\
+\hline
+Compact: $M_{\rm eq}\propto b^0$ & 2.54 & 146.91 \\
+Flat circular speed: $M_{\rm eq}\propto b$ & 2.31 & 79.82 \\
+Constant projected density: $M_{\rm eq}\propto b^2$ & 2.06 & 14.87 \\
+\hline
+\end{tabular}
+\end{center}
+The flat circular-speed row uses the projected radial law of an
+isothermal sphere, not a measurement of a rotation curve. The three
+laws apply over the ray and G1-centroid impact radii only; none is
+proposed as a global mass distribution. In particular, the compact
+trial amplitude is not an estimate of the black hole mass. All
+amplitudes are chosen using the four images. None provides an
+independent prediction or brings those rays to one exact source.
+
+The constant-density trial largely compresses source coordinates;
+ordinary projected mass produces the same optical term. A smaller
+unweighted source-plane scatter therefore does not establish a better
+observational fit or identify a core-coupling signal. To test the proposed
+mechanism, derive the amplitude and radial or angular variation from
+an independently specified core load and its surface response.
+Use image-plane or covariance-aware tests, including resolved host
+structure, and reserve the time delays for a model that passes the
+common-source test. No observed-lens delay is calculated in this screen.
+
+\paragraph{Two checks with no amplitude fit.}
+First, the retained spherical R1 law is tested on a hypothetical
+$10^9 M_\odot$ prescribed point source, keeping its direct Newtonian
+deflection separate. If the point source inherits the ordinary R1
+matter coupling, its screened scalar contribution changes the maximum
+pairwise source separation vector by only $1.47$ microarcseconds.
+This is not a black-hole scalar-charge calculation: the exterior
+equations do not assign a new scalar charge to a horizon.
+
+Second, a spherical exterior-flow candidate assumes
+$g_T=(GM_b/r_s)(1/r-r_s/r^2)$ for $r>r_s$, and zero inside.
+If both weak-field metric potentials receive this same correction,
+the spherical equivalent density is
+$M_b/(4\pi r_s r^2)$ on the exterior. Projection of this density
+is nonzero even for a ray inside $r_s$. For the existing gravity-only
+radius checks, $r_s=8.53$--$13.51$ kpc, and outer cutoffs at $100$ and
+$400$ kpc, it gives $1.25$--$3.66\times10^{10}M_\odot$ in the
+innermost ray cylinder and leaves $735$--$844$ mas of scatter when
+added as a trial correction. These are conditional sensitivity ranges,
+not measurements or a confidence interval. The extended density gives
+the right qualitative shape for a nearly uniform projected lensing
+term, but the tested normalization does not close the rays. Its export
+from a physical core, the physical interface radius, and the metric
+response of the exterior-flow branch remain to be derived.
+
+\paragraph{Calibrating the required load against the conventional halo.}
+The published composite model has an NFW scale radius of $8.43''$ and
+the related environment analysis reports a projected halo fraction of
+about $0.45$ inside the Einstein radius $1.182''$~\cite{Wong2017HE,Sluse2017H0LiCOWII}.
+We use the spherical counterpart as a calibration, not the original
+elliptical reconstruction. With the frozen R1 distances and the circular
+Einstein-aperture mass proxy $\pi R_E^2\Sigma_{\rm crit}$, the halo
+fraction fixes a projected target of $1.50\times10^{11}M_\odot$
+at $R_E=7.03$ kpc. The median scale translates to $r_h=50.1$ kpc.
+A spherical deprojection then specifies
+\begin{equation}
+ g_{\rm cal}(r)=\frac{G M_0}{r^2}\left[
+ \ln(1+x)-\frac{x}{1+x}\right],
+ \qquad x=\frac{r}{r_h},\quad M_0=6.97\times10^{12}M_\odot .
+ \label{eq:he0435-core-calibration}
+\end{equation}
+Here $M_0$ is a profile normalization, not the halo's total mass.
+If metric potentials are equal, the extra inward acceleration
+is $1.62\times10^{-10}\,{\rm m\,s^{-2}}$ at $R_E$.
+The enclosed response $r^2g_{\rm cal}/G$ grows locally as
+$r^{1.83}$ there. A central-only conserved exterior force flux is
+constant, whereas the older flat-speed exterior prescription has
+an enclosed response proportional to $r-r_s$ above its onset.
+The halo calibration therefore identifies the radial response the
+coupled equations must generate, not its cause.
+
+The repository already has a reciprocal attachment condition on
+regular geometry and a collective-curvature interpretation of the
+effective density. The attachment condition matches regular incidence
+data; it does not set the physical load or its curvature conversion
+from a nongeometric core. The optical calibration fixes the projected
+response within the adopted halo model. It does not determine a unique
+three-dimensional topology: a line-of-sight stretch
+$\rho_q(R,z)=\rho_1(R,z/q)/q$ leaves the projected density unchanged
+for every constant $q>0$ in the thin-lens limit.
+Stellar kinematics and independent source paths can test the assumed
+depth and the dynamical response. Passing the calibration itself is not
+validation on those observables, and applying this spherical halo alone
+to the frozen BHSM foreground still leaves about $366$ mas of scatter.
+This is not a replay of the published composite model, which has
+different stellar, halo-shape, and environmental parameters.
